\mt1 Psalms

\c 20

\d For the lead player. A psalm of David.\f + \fr 20:0 \fk KONTEXT \fq For the lead player. A psalm of David \ft The Hebrew superscription is *lamnasseaḥ mizmor lĕdāwid* — a performance instruction naming the music director and assigning the psalm to the Davidic collection. — Psalm 20 belongs to a royal cluster: Psalms 18–21. Psalm 18 is the great retrospective thanksgiving after a past military deliverance. Psalm 19 pivots to Torah and creation. Psalm 20 is the pre-battle liturgy — looking forward to a crisis not yet arrived. Psalm 21 is the thanksgiving after victory. They form a deliberate pair: prayer before, gratitude after. Two psalms further on, Psalm 22 — "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" — inverts Psalm 20's confidence entirely. The Psalter's editors hold both together: trust and abandonment, side by side. — The genre is unusual. Verses 1–5 are not a prayer addressed to God but a blessing-declaration addressed to the king by the congregation. The form is closer to the priestly blessing of Numbers 6:24–26 or Eli's benediction to Hannah ("Go in peace; the God of Israel grant your petition," 1 Samuel 1:17) than to standard psalm-prayer. Imagine it performed: the king has offered his pre-battle sacrifices at the Jerusalem Temple; the people surround him; they pour cascading blessings over him before he departs for war. This is not individual devotion. It is communal liturgy for a specific political-military crisis. — In Jewish tradition, Psalm 20 is recited on Mondays and Thursdays at Shacharit (morning prayer). Midrash Tehillim instructs husbands to recite it twelve times during their wife's labor — the most powerful pre-battle liturgy repurposed for a woman's most vulnerable moment. In Eastern Orthodox tradition, Psalm 20 (numbered Psalm 19 in the LXX) is recited daily at Matins — one of the most-prayed psalms in the entire Christian tradition.\f*

\s1 May the LORD Answer You

\b
\q1
\v 1 May the LORD answer you on the day of trouble.\f + \fr 20:1 \fk KULTUR \fq the Name of Jacob's God \ft "The Name" — Hebrew *šēm* — appears three times in this psalm (vv. 1, 5, 7) and is its architectural center. This is not "God's name" in the way English speakers mean "name" — a label attached from outside. In Israelite theology, the Name of God is an active agent: the covenantal presence of Yahweh made available to those who depend on him. It is almost personified — an efficacious force, not a word. Ancient Ugaritic texts (clay tablets from the thirteenth century BCE) name the goddess Athtart as "the Name of Baal" — a divine attribute functioning as an independent power. Israel took this theological structure and converted it into strict monotheism: the Name of Yahweh is Yahweh himself, present and active for his covenant people. — "Jacob's God" — why Jacob specifically, not Abraham or Isaac? The Midrash Tehillim gives the answer through Rabbi Avin HaLevi: "Abraham had Ishmael; Isaac had Esau; but all of Jacob's children are Israel." The invocation of Jacob's God is an act of radical communal inclusion — this God belongs to the entire people, without exception. The phrase also invokes Jacob's biography: the man who wrestled at Jabbok (Genesis 32), who received the promise at Bethel (Genesis 28), who said "the God who answered me in the day of my distress" (Genesis 35:3) — the same verb, *ʿānāh*, that opens Psalm 20.\f*
\q1 May the Name of Jacob's God lift you beyond reach.

\b
\q1
\v 2 May he send you help from his sanctuary,
\q2 sustain you from Zion.

\b
\q1
\v 3 May he remember all your offerings\f + \fr 20:3 \fk TEXT \fq receive your sacrifice with delight \ft The Hebrew word is *yĕdassheneh* — one of the rarest verbs in the Psalter, from the root *d-š-n* meaning "to be fat, to make rich." The verb appears to mean something like "regard as fat" or "relish" — the language of a deity finding genuine sensory pleasure in the offering. The ancient Greek translation (LXX) could not make sense of it and smoothed it to *euthynai* ("make straight/direct"), losing the gustatory register entirely. Most modern translations flatten it further to "accept" — administratively correct but aesthetically dead. Robert Alter translates "may He relish your burnt offering" — the closest published English rendering to the Hebrew's actual feel. — The background is pre-battle sacrifice: 1 Samuel 7:8–9 and 13:7–9 show the pattern. The king offers burnt offerings (*ʿôlāh*) and grain offerings (*minḥāh*) before departing for war. This is not a purchase of divine favor but a declaration of loyalty and dependence — the king places himself before Yahweh as a supplicant, not a customer. The congregation's prayer that God "receive with delight" asks that the divine response be full-hearted, not merely contractual. — Selah appears in the Hebrew after this verse — a liturgical pause marker, probably indicating a musical interlude or silence in the temple rite.\f*
\q1 and receive your sacrifice with delight.

\b
\q1
\v 4 May he grant you your heart's desire
\q2 and fulfill all your plans.

\b
\q1
\v 5 \add Then\add* we will shout for joy at your deliverance,\f + \fr 20:5 \fk KONTEXT \fq raise our banners in the Name of our God \ft Banner-raising in the ancient Near East was not celebration. It was military communication — a rallying signal and a proclamation of identity. The War Scroll from Qumran (1QM) describes inscribed banners carried before each military unit, bearing divine names and battle slogans. Yigael Yadin documented Ugaritic victory traditions in which the standard functioned as a divine emblem leading the army into battle. To "raise banners in the Name of our God" is to dedicate the military campaign not to a nation or a king but to the divine Name — redirecting the source of confidence. It is a public act of trust-declaration, not a victory dance.\f*
\q1 raise our banners in the Name of our God.
\q1 May the LORD fulfill every request you bring.

\s1 Now I Know

\b
\q1
\v 6 Now I know:\f + \fr 20:6 \fk TEXT \fq his anointed \ft The Hebrew word is *mĕšîḥô* — "his anointed one." This is the word *māšîaḥ*, which English knows as "Messiah" and Greek rendered as *Christos* (Christ). Here it names the Davidic king — the specific historical ruler anointed with oil at his coronation, consecrated for office by the ritual act that transferred royal authority from Yahweh through the prophet or priest. This is not an eschatological title. It is a court title for a living, breathing, mortal king about to march to war. — But the word carries enormous freight. After the destruction of the monarchy in 586 BCE, the word shifted from a present political reference to a future hope: the anointed deliverer yet to come. Jewish tradition read Psalm 20:6 as messianic hope for the nation's restoration. Christian tradition read it as fulfilled in Jesus of Nazareth. Both extensions are real and legitimate — but neither erases the psalm's original meaning. The king is anointed, and the anointing confers accountability, not immunity. The psalm makes the anointed one *dependent* on Yahweh — the most powerful person in Israel stands here as supplicant, not as autonomous sovereign.\f*
\q1 the LORD delivers his anointed.
\q1 He answers him from his holy heavens
\q2 with the saving might of his right hand.

\b
\q1
\v 7 Some trust in chariots, some in horses —\f + \fr 20:7 \fk KULTUR \fq chariots... horses \ft Chariots and horses were the tanks and fighter jets of the ancient world — the most decisive military technology of the Iron Age, possessed by the great empires (Egypt, Assyria, Babylon) and deployed by smaller nations only at enormous cost. Solomon introduced chariot forces to Israel (1 Kings 10:26–29), importing horses from Egypt. The Deuteronomic tradition viewed this as theological compromise: "The king must not acquire many horses for himself, or cause the people to return to Egypt in order to acquire more horses" (Deuteronomy 17:16). Isaiah's prophetic critique is direct: "Woe to those who go down to Egypt for help and rely on horses, who trust in chariots because they are many and in horsemen because they are very strong, but do not look to the Holy One of Israel" (Isaiah 31:1). — Psalm 20:7 is the poetic high-water mark of this tradition. The congregation that prays this psalm likely includes soldiers who will go into battle using chariots and horses. The confession "but we invoke the Name" is made while holding spears. The anti-chariot declaration is not pacifism — it is trust relocation. Victory may be real, but its source is not in hardware. The psalm does not say "we have no chariots." It says "we do not trust in them." — The verse's structure is deliberately periodic: "These — through chariots; these — through horses; but WE — in the Name of the LORD our God — we invoke." The verb that gives everything meaning (*nazkîr*, "we invoke") is withheld until the end. The sentence structure performs the waiting it describes: everything hangs on the final word.\f*
\q1 but we invoke the Name of the LORD our God.

\b
\q1
\v 8 They crumple. They fall.
\q1 But we — we rise. We stand firm.

\b
\q1
\v 9 LORD, deliver.\f + \fr 20:9 \fk TEXT \fq answer us \ft This verse closes the psalm's structural arch — and the pronoun shift from "you" (v.1) to "us" (v.9) is its most moving architectural move. In verse 1, the congregation blessed the king: "May the LORD answer *you*." Here, nine verses later, they pray: "answer *us* on the day *we* call." What has happened in between is a quiet revolution. The congregation has absorbed the king's crisis into their own. They began as spectators praying for another; they end as participants in the same dependence. The king's vulnerability has become communal vulnerability. — The Hebrew verb *ʿānāh* (answer) appears in v.1, v.6, and v.9 — binding the psalm together with a three-fold repetition. The word has covenantal weight: God's "answering" is not merely informational. It is active intervention — the response of a covenant partner to the cry of one who depends on the covenant. — Jewish and Christian spiritual traditions have both read this solidarity movement as the psalm's central invitation: intercession transforms the intercessor. You cannot pray for someone in crisis without entering the crisis yourself. By the time you reach "answer us," you have discovered that their ordeal is yours.\f*
\q1 Answer us on the day we call.
